  "worked_solution": "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$",
